{"context": "A research team is studying an airline customer-service agent benchmark to determine whether the benchmark under-elicits agent capability. They have identified three hypothesized sources of under-elicitation: errors in the simulated database, gold answer actions inconsistent with the stated policy, and ambiguous instructions to the simulated user. They have an execution-and-logging harness and automated log analysis tools available. They now need to surface which benchmark tasks are likely flawed and gather evidence characterizing the flaws.\n\nQuestion: How should the team identify which benchmark tasks are likely to contain errors, and what evidence should they collect to characterize each flaw?", "options": ["Replay suspect tasks through the harness, gather full trajectories where grading diverges from expected behavior, and tag each flaw with which hypothesis it supports.", "Scan agent logs for tasks with very low success across all agents and relatively higher failure rates for stronger agents, then use those patterns to flag flaws."], "principles": [], "persona": "An AI safety researcher or technical forecaster who prizes epistemic rigor, precise conceptual distinctions, and methodological soundness. They insist on clear definitional boundaries—scheming vs. reward hacking, distinct task capabilities vs. single capability estimates—over tidy collapsible taxonomies. They favor theory-first reasoning (coverage claims need defined criteria) paired with concrete, mechanism-focused empirical evidence over speculative arguments. They are scope-disciplined: they prefer tractable, well-founded analyses over ambitious extensions built on noisy inputs, and conservative, hedged forecasts that flag model limitations over confident headline numbers. On alignment risk, they take deceptive alignment seriously as an incentive-structure concern while remaining cautious about unproven claims, favoring layered defenses (control, interpretability, oversight), mechanism-aware measurement, and mitigations that don't merely train models to hide the measured signal. They distrust lab-to-deployment extrapolation, preferring real-world data even if noisy. They are skeptical of low-evidence-value experiments with confounded interpretations, preferring controlled baselines, non-IID generalization tests, and diagnostics that cleanly distinguish hypotheses. They value conceptual generality (architecture-agnostic interpretability, universal learning dynamics) and calm, accessible public communication.", "past_choices": []}
